Nicole:<br><br>I&#39;m afraid it is not quite as simple as that.  You&#39;ll note that the line<br>of code that you wish to change (the one that creates object dsm.44)<br>make a call to the function dsm.fit().  If you have a look at that<br>function, you will see that function in turn calls the function gam()<br>from the mgcv package (because the argument &#39;fn&#39; requests the fit of a gam).<br><br>If you explore the R documentation for gam() in mgcv, you&#39;ll be directed<br>to the description of &#39;family&#39; describing how you specify the family of<br>the error structure for your model.  Tweedie is not one of the choices<br>available to you in the &#39;family&#39; argument to gam().<br><br>Instead you will need to investigate how to fit your model not using the<br>dsm.fit() function, but rather working from the constituent R objects<br>(sample.dat.44 and obs.dat.44) you will build the structure necessary to<br>fit your model with the error structure you desire.  I&#39;ve not worked<br>with fitting models with the Tweedie error structure, so I can&#39;t<br>describe how it does work.<br><br>Nicole Koshure wrote:<br>&gt; &gt; I have generated R code using the &quot;debug&quot;mode in Distance. The code is created by the DSM engine. Currently Distance only allows you to select from a few distribution families when using the DSM engine. Unfortunately I have many segments in my data set with no sightings - resulting in a very zero-inflated data set.<br>&gt; &gt;<br>&gt; &gt; I would like to try to fit the DSM using a Tweedie distribution - which would be more appropriate for fitting a data set which is zero-inflated. I am however having a difficult time with the R code for this.<br>&gt; &gt;<br>&gt; &gt; Here is the code generated by Distance from the DSM with a &quot;quasipoisson&quot; family:<br>&gt; &gt;<br>&gt; &gt; if(sum(.packages(all=T)==&#39;dsm&#39;)==0)<br>&gt; &gt;   install.packages(&#39;C:\Program Files\Distance 6\dsm.zip&#39;, repos=NULL)<br>&gt; &gt; library(dsm)<br>&gt; &gt; file.create(&#39;C:\DOCUME~1\NICOLE~1\LOCALS~1\Temp\dst65008\res.r&#39;)<br>&gt; &gt; file.create(&#39;C:\DOCUME~1\NICOLE~1\LOCALS~1\Temp\dst65008\stat.r&#39;)<br>&gt; &gt; source(&#39;C:\Program Files\Distance 6\support.r&#39;)<br>&gt; &gt; sample.dat.44&lt;-read.table(file=&#39;C:\DOCUME~1\NICOLE~1\LOCALS~1\Temp\dst65008\sample.dat.r&#39;, header=TRUE, sep=&#39;	&#39;, comment.char=&#39;&#39;)<br>&gt; &gt; obs.dat.44&lt;-read.table(file=&#39;C:\DOCUME~1\NICOLE~1\LOCALS~1\Temp\dst65008\obs.dat.r&#39;, header=TRUE, sep=&#39;	&#39;, comment.char=&#39;&#39;)<br>&gt; &gt; dsm.44&lt;-dsm.fit(ddfobject=ddf.2,response=&#39;indiv.est&#39;,model.defn=list(fn=&#39;gam&#39;, family=&#39;quasipoisson&#39;),wghts=NULL,off=covered,link=&#39;log&#39;,formula=te(midlat,midlon),obsdata=obs.dat.44,segdata=sample.dat.44,convert.units=1)<br>&gt; &gt;<br>&gt; &gt; #theoretically I should be able to change the family from quasipoisson to tweedie in the above line of code but this does not appear to work - even when assigning a p value. I am hoping to use a pvalue of 1.1 for the tweedie. (Tweedie(1.1))<br>&gt; &gt; Note: I have also loaded the Tweedie package and all other required packages in R to use a Tweedie Distribution<br>&gt; &gt;<br>&gt; &gt; # below is the remaining code<br>&gt; &gt; sink(file=&#39;C:\DOCUME~1\NICOLE~1\LOCALS~1\Temp\dst65008\res.r&#39;,append=T)<br>&gt; &gt; cat(&#39;	Response Surface/Summary	
&#39;)<br>&gt; &gt; summary(dsm.44$result)<br>&gt; &gt; bmp(filename=&#39;gamcheck44.%02d.bmp&#39;,width=480,height=340,pointsize=12)<br>&gt; &gt; gam.check(dsm.44$result)<br>&gt; &gt; cat(&#39;	Response Surface/Plot: gam-check	
&#39;)<br>&gt; &gt; dev.off()<br>&gt; &gt; bmp(filename=&#39;resid44.%02d.bmp&#39;,width=480,height=340,pointsize=12)<br>&gt; &gt; plot(dsm.44$result$fitted.values,residuals(dsm.44$result,type=&#39;deviance&#39;), xlab=&#39;Fitted&#39;, ylab=&#39;Deviance Residuals&#39;)<br>&gt; &gt; cat(&#39;	Response Surface/Plot: residual vs fitted	
&#39;)<br>&gt; &gt; dev.off()<br>&gt; &gt; bmp(filename=&#39;diagnose44.%02d.bmp&#39;,width=480,height=340,pointsize=12)<br>&gt; &gt; plot(dsm.44$result, pages=, scale=0)<br>&gt; &gt; dev.off()<br>&gt; &gt; cat(&#39;	Response Surface/Plot: Diagnostic-Plot	
&#39;)<br>&gt; &gt; sink()<br>&gt; &gt; sink(file=&#39;C:\DOCUME~1\NICOLE~1\LOCALS~1\Temp\dst65008\stat.r&#39;,append=T)<br>&gt; &gt; dsm.summary &lt;- summary(dsm.44$result)<br>&gt; &gt; cat(&#39;5010&#39;, dsm.summary$sp.criterion, &#39;
&#39;)<br>&gt; &gt; cat(&#39;5020&#39;, dsm.summary$n, &#39;
&#39;)<br>&gt; &gt; cat(&#39;5030&#39;, dsm.summary$dev.expl, &#39;
&#39;)<br>&gt; &gt; cat(&#39;5040&#39;, dsm.summary$scale, &#39;
&#39;)<br>&gt; &gt; sink()<br>&gt; &gt; rm(dsm.summary)<br>&gt; &gt;<br>&gt; &gt; If any of you are wizards at R or have attempted to do something similar with your data I would love to hear from you.<br>&gt; &gt;<br>&gt; &gt; Any suggestions or code which works would be greatly appreciated!<br>&gt; &gt;<br>&gt; &gt; Thank you<br>&gt; &gt;<br>&gt; &gt; Nicole<br>
